---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout title="Canvas & Rendering" description="Varve's canvas: infinite canvas with culling, sticky snap, rulers, guides, grids, view rotation, multi-page documents, and a live minimap, rendered by a native Rust engine.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Canvas & Rendering
      </nav>

      <h1>Canvas & Rendering</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        Responsive IR-replay rendering with an infinite canvas, precision tools, and native Rust performance.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>What It Is</h2>
        <p>
          Varve's canvas system uses IR-replay architecture: the Rust engine computes the scene and emits a compact intermediate representation (~42 KB/frame for 600 shapes), which the webview replays to canvas2D. A historical Wayland fixture measured 86fps with this approach, compared with 8.5fps for the pixel-pushing comparison; actual performance depends on the document, renderer, and device.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section responsive-contract" data-testid="canvas-responsiveness-contract">
        <h2>Responsive under the hand</h2>
        <p>
          Panels opening, windows resizing, browser chrome moving, and display scale changes update the drawable canvas geometry without changing the document. The camera preserves the world point under the viewport centre, or under the user's active pointer when a gesture is in progress, so the artwork does not jump away from the hand.
        </p>
        <div class="responsive-contract__grid">
          <article>
            <h3>Stable coordinates</h3>
            <p>Pointer and wheel input use the latest client-space canvas position before converting to world space.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Continuous interaction</h3>
            <p>Resize observation is coalesced, while pointer capture, blur, and cancellation paths clear stale gesture state.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Honest rendering</h3>
            <p>Fast paths remain optional. If a fresh worker frame is not available, the authoritative replay path is used instead of showing stale pixels.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="responsive-contract__evidence">
          This contract is exercised by the canvas browser regressions and documented in the <a href="https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/blob/main/docs/audits/canvas-responsiveness-2026-09-08.md">canvas responsiveness audit</a>. Performance figures remain fixture-specific rather than a promise for every document or device.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section" data-testid="canvas-label-scope">
        <h2>Labels follow the surface</h2>
        <p>
          Canvas names and object labels belong to the Design Canvas you are viewing. Switch
          canvases and the labels, accessibility names, minimap, selection, and hit targets switch
          with the artwork — a nearby canvas with the same coordinates or a duplicate name cannot
          leak into the current view.
        </p>
        <p>
          Print uses a separate publishing-scene policy: page number and name bands describe
          placed pages, while Design Canvas content stays out of the print pasteboard. These
          editing labels are derived interface chrome, so they do not become document nodes or
          appear in PNG, SVG, PDF, video, clipboard, or other exports.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section" data-testid="canvas-precision-movement">
        <h2>Precision movement</h2>
        <p>
          Nudge frames and objects from the keyboard without changing tools: once the canvas has
          focus, Arrow and Shift+Arrow use your configured small and big document-unit amounts.
          Movement stays aligned to world axes through zoom, camera rotation, and transformed
          parents, while focused fields and editing widgets keep their own Arrow-key behavior.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pointer dragging shares the same hierarchy-safe movement roots, keeps multi-selection
          rigid, and retains snapping and intentional drag reparenting as separate behaviors.
          Use Ctrl/Cmd to bypass snapping or Ctrl/Cmd+Shift to preserve the current parent.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Images stay local and editable</h2>
        <p>
          Embedded images are stored once in the document and shared by every placement. Crops, fit, rotation, flips, masks, and treatments remain per-use edits, while the canvas resolves the same source through its local decode cache on desktop and web. Payload-free asset references are repaired at document and render boundaries, so reopening or switching workspace does not turn an image into a grey placeholder. Home previews use that same canonical scene pipeline and can rebuild their disposable cache from the saved document when an older preview is missing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <figure class="feature-visual">
        <img
          src={sitePath('/screenshots/layout-light.png')}
          alt="Two pages of a Varve document side by side on the canvas, with page labels and ruler guides showing precise positioning"
          width="1440"
          height="900"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Multiple pages, rulers, and precision tools on one infinite canvas</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Key Capabilities</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Infinite canvas:</strong> Floating origin camera system for unlimited workspace</li>
          <li><strong>Viewport culling:</strong> Only render what's visible for performance at scale</li>
          <li><strong>Dirty-rect redraw:</strong> Minimize redraws to changed regions</li>
          <li><strong>Precision tools:</strong> Sticky snap, numerical precision, sub-pixel accuracy</li>
          <li><strong>Keyboard nudging:</strong> Configurable small/big movement amounts with world-axis semantics and active-tool fallback</li>
          <li><strong>Multi-page support:</strong> Work with multiple artboards/pages in one document</li>
          <li><strong>Live minimap:</strong> See the active design canvas or publishing pasteboard, then click or drag to navigate without changing your artwork</li>
          <li><strong>Surface-aware labels:</strong> Keep frame names readable and local to the active canvas, with accessible names and safe long-name handling</li>
          <li><strong>Rulers and guides:</strong> Visual guides for alignment and measurement</li>
          <li><strong>Grid overlays:</strong> Configurable grid patterns for precision work</li>
          <li><strong>Local image assets:</strong> Embedded image sources stay in the document, decode progressively, and remain editable through crop, fit, masks, and treatments</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Technical Depth</h2>
        <p>
          The rendering pipeline is built on IR-replay (ADR-0001), validated empirically on Wayland Linux. The engine uses viewport culling, dirty-rect redraw, and spatial indexing for fast hit testing. The SubtreeReplayCache optimizes repeated rendering of unchanged subtrees. The minimap reuses the same world bounds, page placement, camera rotation, and live canvas dimensions as the main surface, so navigation remains an honest overview rather than a second approximation of the document.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same facade works on desktop (native Rust via Tauri IPC) and web (WASM), with the browser-side renderer retaining an authoritative replay fallback when an accelerated or worker path cannot provide a fresh frame.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Limitations</h2>
        <div class="limitations">
          <p><strong>Current limitations:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Some legacy integrations still expose world-space values; use the artboard-local conversion helpers for page-aware tools and exports</li>
            <li>Collaboration features are UI scaffolding only (no transport or wire protocol)</li>
            <li>Responsive behavior is validated in automated Chromium viewports; physical display scaling, compositor scheduling, and unusually large documents can change the observed frame time</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Related Documentation</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/docs/architecture')}>Architecture Overview</a> - IR-replay architecture details</li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/docs/rendering')}>Rendering Pipeline</a> - Canvas system internals</li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/vector')}>Vector Tools</a> - Selection and movement controls</li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/blob/main/docs/architecture/render-pipeline.md">Render Pipeline Documentation</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <h2>Ready to try it?</h2>
        <a href={sitePath('/download')} class="btn-default">Download Varve</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }
  
  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  
  .breadcrumb a {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  
  .feature-page h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.5rem);
    font-family: var(--font-editorial);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
  
  .feature-intro {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  
  .feature-section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  
  .feature-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
  
  .feature-section p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  
  .feature-section ul {
    list-style: none;
  }
  
  .feature-section li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    position: relative;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  
  .feature-section li::before {
    content: "•";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--text-link-hover);
  }
  
  .feature-section strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .responsive-contract {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
  }

  .responsive-contract__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .responsive-contract__grid article {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
    background: var(--surface-default);
  }

  .responsive-contract__grid h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .responsive-contract__grid p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .responsive-contract__evidence {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .feature-visual {
    margin: -1rem 0 3rem;
  }

  .feature-visual img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-shadow-raised);
  }

  .feature-visual figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .limitations {
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-card);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
  
  .limitations p {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  
  .feature-cta {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 3rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default);
  }
  
  .feature-cta h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .feature-page {
      padding: 2rem 0;
    }

    .responsive-contract {
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .responsive-contract__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
